From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: "Claude (agent session)" <darren@obot.ai>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 23:43:00 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] renderer/d3d11: set the viewport in RenderPass.begin

Nothing the pipelines drew was ever visible. D3D11 starts with no viewport
bound, and the only function that called RSSetViewports - Device.bindRenderTarget
- had no callers at all.

This failed in the most misleading way available: ClearRenderTargetView ignores
the viewport, so the surface cleared correctly and looked healthy, while every
draw was clipped away in its entirety. The backend submitted a full frame each
time (bg_color, cell_bg, and cell_text with ~98 instanced glyph quads, all with
valid shaders, buffers and atlas textures bound) and produced no pixels, with
no D3D error anywhere, because being clipped by an empty viewport is not an
error.

The viewport now comes from the first attachment rather than the device size,
so render passes that target an offscreen texture - the post-processing chain -
get the texture's dimensions instead of the swapchain's.

Verified by making bg_color output magenta and capturing the window: the client
area went fully magenta with the cmd.exe banner, prompt and cursor drawn over
it in JetBrains Mono. Reverted, the same window renders the shell on the normal
dark background.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
 src/renderer/directx11/RenderPass.zig | 24 ++++++++++++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 24 insertions(+)

diff --git a/src/renderer/directx11/RenderPass.zig b/src/renderer/directx11/RenderPass.zig
index 35098262d..23083c379 100644
--- a/src/renderer/directx11/RenderPass.zig
+++ b/src/renderer/directx11/RenderPass.zig
@@ -122,6 +122,30 @@ pub fn begin(opts: Options) Self {
 
     ctx.OMSetRenderTargets(rtvs[0..count], null);
 
+    // Set the viewport to match the first attachment.
+    //
+    // This is not optional and its absence is not obvious: D3D11 starts with
+    // no viewport bound, and `ClearRenderTargetView` ignores the viewport
+    // while every draw is clipped entirely by it. A missing viewport
+    // therefore looks exactly like a correctly cleared surface that refuses
+    // to draw anything - which is precisely what it did, with the pipelines
+    // submitting geometry every frame and nothing appearing.
+    if (opts.attachments.len > 0) {
+        const dims: struct { w: usize, h: usize } = switch (opts.attachments[0].target) {
+            .target => |t| .{ .w = t.width, .h = t.height },
+            .texture => |t| .{ .w = t.width, .h = t.height },
+        };
+        const viewport: d3d11.D3D11_VIEWPORT = .{
+            .TopLeftX = 0,
+            .TopLeftY = 0,
+            .Width = @floatFromInt(dims.w),
+            .Height = @floatFromInt(dims.h),
+            .MinDepth = 0,
+            .MaxDepth = 1,
+        };
+        ctx.RSSetViewports(&.{viewport});
+    }
+
     return .{ .api = opts.api, .attachments = opts.attachments };
 }
 
